# Solution 17.1 — the sigil goes on `has_unsafe_successor`.
#
# `safe` is the predicate you are trying to establish, so it is the one that
# should have to earn its tuples: minimal, starting empty. The helper is the
# counterexample finder, so it is the anti-monotone side and takes the `^`.
#
# Putting the sigil on `safe` instead is rejected outright here, because
# `sink(N)` and `has_successor(N)` are positive calls into predicates outside
# the cycle -- fine -- but `safe` would then be maximal while
# `has_unsafe_successor` stayed minimal only if you moved *both* markers. Move
# both and the program is accepted but answers a different question: every
# node is safe until something proves otherwise, so a cycle of nodes pointing
# at each other would come out safe rather than unsafe.

node("a"). node("b"). node("c"). node("bad").
edge("a", "b"). edge("b", "c"). edge("a", "bad").

sink(N) :- node(N), not has_successor(N).
has_successor(N) :- edge(N, _).

safe(N) :- sink(N), N <> "bad".
safe(N) :- has_successor(N), not has_unsafe_successor^(N).

has_unsafe_successor^(N) :- edge(N, M), not safe(M).

# b and c are safe; a is not, because it can reach `bad`.
?- safe(N).
